      en: "Mont Gréboun, with an elevation of approximately 1,944 meters, is one of the highest peaks in the Aïr Mountains of northern Niger and stands as a prominent landmark in the central Sahara. Geologically, the mountain is part of a massive Precambrian granite intrusion complex, further shaped by volcanic activity during the Tertiary period. Its steep flanks and rugged rock formations offer a spectacular sight amidst the barren desert landscape. Despite the extreme climatic conditions, Mont Gréboun hosts relict vegetation in its protected canyons, pointing to a wetter past in the Sahara, including rare wild olive trees and Mediterranean plant species. Historically, the mountain massif served as a retreat for Tuareg groups and is rich in archaeological sites, including Neolithic rock engravings. Climbing the summit is considered a significant challenge for mountaineers due to extreme heat and remoteness. Today, Mont Gréboun is a symbol of the rugged beauty of the Aïr and is part of the UNESCO World Heritage listed Aïr and Ténéré Natural Reserve.",
      es: "El Mont Gréboun se eleva como una de las cumbres más importantes e imponentes, alzándose majestuosamente sobre el paisaje áspero de las volcánicas montañas de Aïr. Con una impresionante altura de 1.944 metros, esta montaña oscura y escarpada ofrece una vista panorámica espectacular sobre el infinito mar de arena del Sahara oriental. Los flancos inferiores de la montaña ocultan gargantas profundas y sombreadas, donde crecen raras acacias y los zorros del desierto encuentran refugio. Para los montañeros y geólogos audaces, el Mont Gréboun representa uno de los desafíos más atractivos pero también exigentes de toda África Occidental, debido a sus macizas formaciones de basalto y su ubicación aislada.",
